69" :question/math-academy-id "q-110127" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/e09f5a90c683876f849577cd75af3c77cc21c6f9af10c5a4ece47718aa33f738.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,4)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(4,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(4,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(4)=2.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $-\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110127/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-110127"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-110198/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" :answer/id #uuid "fc9bf329-1eed-55c7-a672-f78fbd363769" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110198/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "014896cb-38e4-55f7-835b-62f9fbf455dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110198/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "f9a28e06-914c-5135-870a-9a7021354620" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110198/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "7fd6599b-4203-5576-ab15-1332d294ab7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110198/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "b55bcc0f-a090-5ff4-929c-931a4f7d99c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110198/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "068b4da3-a2e0-5ae7-a4bd-7cb5bd0c479f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-110198/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer-field/choices ["q-110198/selection/3a619fab2616dab0463f9c76cb72cd9f101e45e62111356a5b0b111e062a8dcf" "q-110198/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" "q-110198/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-110198/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-110198/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-110198" :question/id #uuid "897b7921-24ed-546d-9505-bfabbd58980a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110198" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/ba762e13c4015b43b99f9038ee63a247584345cd535e12b80c1f3b0d9e50e70b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(0,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,2]$ is $-2$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110198/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-110198"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342575/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "67da14c0-cb28-53af-8e70-84ccda2d27fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342575/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4cc73e86-36af-52ce-a446-19f7fb18768d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342575/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer-field/choices ["q-342575/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342575" :question/id #uuid "337a9944-8b6d-5e9a-8280-a4fd810a5d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-342575" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/ce948d3eade308a90ffa85a7c7a88a8c403a6620ffda7e6d24444d2abac0770c.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(1,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(1,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [-2,4]$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342575/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342575"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342561/field-1/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "d8d9e563-e33c-5037-b3ea-82ea794b6308" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342561/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "285d5e04-6dab-56cc-8d98-a8430a74c834" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342561/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "19491414-5359-58d0-962c-8d3a1746c460" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342561/field-1/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer-field/choices ["q-342561/field-1/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-342561/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342561" :question/id #uuid "7fd6ddaf-3c46-5030-b4a5-c0761f64013d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342561" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/adcee8bddcbbd96e18d63f85f5b9ebf2edc52897cc1ddcca7e377194462f6bda.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(-1,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(0,-1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(-1)=3,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $B\\,(0,-1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=-1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(-1)=3.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342561/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342561"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-110129/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "a09bbb6b-847b-59b6-99a1-f194c396a855" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110129/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "a9168eb8-94d2-5d8c-bdef-8759c2bfb850" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110129/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "1aa292b8-4ad7-5bbd-8258-0c90f60206b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110129/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "481f5132-0309-5f94-b274-f3716fefb545" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110129/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "2d973d9f-7362-51c6-b8bf-1955272edfe3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110129/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "27e44538-9754-50ca-9436-c89b95106aaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-110129/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-110129/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-110129/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-110129/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-110129/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-110129/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-110129" :question/id #uuid "30b07781-82ca-5995-998f-b7f04d3afb4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110129" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/265866e56e07a86dd4d6f43ce5d752d62491dea962c3dce3273b924e6e2b362b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,3)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(3,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(3,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(3)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,2.5]$ is $2$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110129/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-110129"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342585/field-1/a2301b8122c312044fb5dc0b0c60c599b4f2f7f4ffcc198c9893873ab9165c9d" :answer/id #uuid "13dfb1f2-a005-5d13-ae89-04d337e5c46d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342585/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f44b7b1-53a5-502c-9ed2-d2363703b510" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342585/field-1/a2301b8122c312044fb5dc0b0c60c599b4f2f7f4ffcc198c9893873ab9165c9d" :answer-field/choices ["q-342585/field-1/a2301b8122c312044fb5dc0b0c60c599b4f2f7f4ffcc198c9893873ab9165c9d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342585" :question/id #uuid "0d9fadce-8516-5492-be9d-1ecb20a64fee" :question/math-academy-id "q-342585" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and the point $P(e,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(e,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(e,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,e)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(e)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{e})} \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342585/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342585"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342579/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "38f06589-d04c-547d-a8f1-0ce386c86214" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342579/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "2a0c5b6b-e340-5575-8040-e67e50780fa6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342579/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "32abd2ef-a001-5271-8715-9a083d0a399d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342579/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-342579/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-342579/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342579" :question/id #uuid "1feeaa24-053b-5b18-a92c-a34f2428102b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342579" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and the point $P(1,0)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,0).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot {({x}^{2}+1)}^{′}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}+2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1+2x-{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1+2(1)-{1}^{2}}{{({1}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-342579/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342579"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342580/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "709724cf-a5c4-5518-9d05-d0e382847be2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342580/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e494bbd2-f727-5bb1-be2d-e7c49a134540" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342580/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-342580/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342580" :question/id #uuid "2b4427d3-b344-5045-8e56-9bfadf272756" :question/math-academy-id "q-342580" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\sqrt[3]{x}$ and the point $P(8,6)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(8,6).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{8}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(8,6)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(6,8)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342580/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342580"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-15652/selection/e1cda0cf0a7219f9a5c1b48ea1a1fff8481245f1236a91f878fbff739c02508a" :answer/id #uuid "311e8ecc-4b5d-58be-b482-3bbae10a1e7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15652/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "a8edbfcb-1b92-512b-aa38-0c2aafb2eb8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15652/selection/852df530a82e413447a8b733db5e62e6c70e6d0be8b2e885cd815d17a6fb4ba1" :answer/id #uuid "dc1915c9-6c2b-55c3-b5b0-5df88ed581d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15652/selection/ac46d580a95a8fc03d1a7cb07de70e107b73fba6bf1bc1d13103b84eff3df012" :answer/id #uuid "2d5bae6b-20ac-538e-891d-8254734ca436" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15652/selection/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" :answer/id #uuid "66c87dd3-1c92-52cb-92c3-5a09eb773c49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15652/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc98d4c2-2902-5abf-9024-1bb5676fba2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-15652/selection/e1cda0cf0a7219f9a5c1b48ea1a1fff8481245f1236a91f878fbff739c02508a" :answer-field/choices ["q-15652/selection/e1cda0cf0a7219f9a5c1b48ea1a1fff8481245f1236a91f878fbff739c02508a" "q-15652/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-15652/selection/852df530a82e413447a8b733db5e62e6c70e6d0be8b2e885cd815d17a6fb4ba1" "q-15652/selection/ac46d580a95a8fc03d1a7cb07de70e107b73fba6bf1bc1d13103b84eff3df012" "q-15652/selection/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-15652" :question/id #uuid "71816a61-baa5-5230-bd29-159dd6440cd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-15652" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3+4x}{2-3x}$ and the point $P(1,-7)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(-7,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,-7).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3+4x}{2-3x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{17}{(2-3x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =17\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,-7)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-7,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-7) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-15652/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-15652"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-15651/selection/5c0598f19f8324c341654ad8fd4df667dbb52f406431a30b6711906dc66d3ee4" :answer/id #uuid "5a6f4abc-bea0-51c9-8e2d-5e031ab0c129" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15651/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "08cc8d48-106c-514d-9cb5-2fa415f7034d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15651/selection/f20b1040970d812ca8a23b6aa5caeb5fdcc2d68c71091093d124a8107e35e06b" :answer/id #uuid "0d8aceb1-18dd-5a1d-9de1-cc181dfa7657" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{32}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15651/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer/id #uuid "3df97db9-3edc-5e56-9da4-33f6081aac5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15651/selection/1966d8255529bacb489fa43aedb24b3fa9beeaaa2157768264b90df29243932a" :answer/id #uuid "2214a2ef-dfcd-59d9-954f-6871162ceb57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15651/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "65c78d50-f7fb-52d7-96cb-16cbbe422d3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-15651/selection/1966d8255529bacb489fa43aedb24b3fa9beeaaa2157768264b90df29243932a" :answer-field/choices ["q-15651/selection/5c0598f19f8324c341654ad8fd4df667dbb52f406431a30b6711906dc66d3ee4" "q-15651/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" "q-15651/selection/f20b1040970d812ca8a23b6aa5caeb5fdcc2d68c71091093d124a8107e35e06b" "q-15651/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" "q-15651/selection/1966d8255529bacb489fa43aedb24b3fa9beeaaa2157768264b90df29243932a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-15651" :question/id #uuid "9defc19b-b540-5a93-90de-15be5571aa37" :question/math-academy-id "q-15651" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}$ and the point $P(7,\\frac{3}{4})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{3}{4},7).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(7,\\frac{3}{4}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x-3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{4(x-3)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(7) & =-\\frac{3}{32}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(7,\\frac{3}{4})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{3}{4},7)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(\\frac{3}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{4}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{32})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-15651/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-15651"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-4962/selection/3a3e10526f40c673b7703a3c103fe7589223997acb2a083d0e3a20b742c55555" :answer/id #uuid "e6cf7dbb-640b-5e4e-a3c6-8fb004e30a86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4962/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "6bd22d9a-a03c-58f5-9efb-0242510aa540" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4962/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "88764d7d-1cd8-51c9-8f83-e7a18a77daf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4962/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e20