ifficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-4962/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-4962"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-110272/selection/e1122882be3ccd3a3d2b1d68d35a0154503fc8f6a613def1d97f79d0c3a01a0d" :answer/id #uuid "88412c1d-bd9b-5346-aa51-252c52f9ee28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110272/selection/7053332ec0382107a7a719ab8e24a1e83dac371355afe58aeed0ee373af936a9" :answer/id #uuid "eca4998f-0622-5cfb-8cc2-b8c825273700" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{46}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110272/selection/db3babdc233009c9211f7b76bc366670e92fb1a2f2a8c5ccfb158ca3e98ca8b2" :answer/id #uuid "75dbe5e6-9df1-5920-a28a-bcb0687ff554" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110272/selection/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49" :answer/id #uuid "242b6e9e-1054-54f8-884f-faec3ec6194c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110272/selection/29cc2f60e638121dd3fb8bc1724b0279bf17e7592b74b565aa2956d4b2014422" :answer/id #uuid "8cc2dbe6-2683-5c50-9b19-63fd9174cf06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{31}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110272/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a46c9094-ba55-5f98-a3f1-1d3c3bedbc27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-110272/selection/29cc2f60e638121dd3fb8bc1724b0279bf17e7592b74b565aa2956d4b2014422" :answer-field/choices ["q-110272/selection/e1122882be3ccd3a3d2b1d68d35a0154503fc8f6a613def1d97f79d0c3a01a0d" "q-110272/selection/7053332ec0382107a7a719ab8e24a1e83dac371355afe58aeed0ee373af936a9" "q-110272/selection/db3babdc233009c9211f7b76bc366670e92fb1a2f2a8c5ccfb158ca3e98ca8b2" "q-110272/selection/66dedd51c696580d351ab6a45c1c63cb495081ccf219c3c3c62f1d90fbeb5a49" "q-110272/selection/29cc2f60e638121dd3fb8bc1724b0279bf17e7592b74b565aa2956d4b2014422"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-110272" :question/id #uuid "75bdddd1-2f9e-5cb2-9924-d4847b6838c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-110272" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $5$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4)=\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}+{g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=3,$ $g(4)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(4)=5,$ and ${f}^{′}(3)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4) & =\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}+{g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{f}^{′}(3)}+5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2}+15 \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110272/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-110272"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342596/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "44ff3980-1fed-50f1-9128-e3ca6632b8ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342596/field-1/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "bff14158-2799-57d3-9c92-9753e0e65652" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342596/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "630d3a62-d1f0-5ad8-b11f-df4ca1ba102f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342596/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer-field/choices ["q-342596/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" "q-342596/field-1/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342596" :question/id #uuid "0b938f4e-7aed-514c-aa88-1f8816849a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-342596" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=1,$ $g(2)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342596/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342596"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75834/selection/fcc08b5493ae6f7b4bc0aad6b1791b974d36d4679ce4f932434b29e95cebea06" :answer/id #uuid "d47c6600-01dc-5d33-b6e8-011f79801de1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75834/selection/71b1db0b2a675655ec27a2d2ceef2e86712a68eb78548232215378e0a354fb4c" :answer/id #uuid "00215d8d-3c88-53a5-b3f6-f6544c1b3deb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75834/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" :answer/id #uuid "11ab7dd8-1ed6-5d53-896b-c3a66f271c63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75834/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "b1ad5966-2d46-50b5-9119-6be2b62c4917" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75834/selection/aa640bdf1bd5b2b15c3dbfe770f68b41d304d610e4d1af786c1f1372fe3dc48f" :answer/id #uuid "988022e4-7957-5221-a489-a318fdb6b512" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75834/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0b3347d-fb13-5fdf-8c5e-5cbc7ca101dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75834/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" :answer-field/choices ["q-75834/selection/fcc08b5493ae6f7b4bc0aad6b1791b974d36d4679ce4f932434b29e95cebea06" "q-75834/selection/71b1db0b2a675655ec27a2d2ceef2e86712a68eb78548232215378e0a354fb4c" "q-75834/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" "q-75834/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" "q-75834/selection/aa640bdf1bd5b2b15c3dbfe770f68b41d304d610e4d1af786c1f1372fe3dc48f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75834" :question/id #uuid "6b67208f-6f32-509e-9ceb-61d4d53fe2b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75834" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({g}^{-1})^{′}(2).$\n\n| **$x$** | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $-6$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ | $-5$ |\n| $7$ | $-2$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${g}^{-1}(2)=5$ and ${g}^{′}(5)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({g}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-75834/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75834"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-312584/field-1/f114c855cb4980abfc1057e37678c04cac7ea9d9ad649da669e46dcd5213df25" :answer/id #uuid "ddcd8470-cb6e-52a8-baa6-16cfad6bf91c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-312584/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "63e79ecb-5a2b-5edd-b93c-554db2be15a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-312584/field-1/f114c855cb4980abfc1057e37678c04cac7ea9d9ad649da669e46dcd5213df25" :answer-field/choices ["q-312584/field-1/f114c855cb4980abfc1057e37678c04cac7ea9d9ad649da669e46dcd5213df25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-312584" :question/id #uuid "74b2f2fb-bda2-5fab-bba0-63ecfa763a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-312584" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $4$ | $-3$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=5,$ $g(2)=4,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=-3,$ and ${f}^{′}(5)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 5+\\frac{4}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =-15+\\frac{4}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-15-4 \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-312584/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-312584"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-312589/field-1/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46" :answer/id #uuid "0cbece89-1b26-5184-9d69-8c70945c64c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-312589/field-1/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "2a454b79-7424-5cc5-9a21-f341ebc41a83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-312589/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b3a9d6f6-b957-5ea4-80e1-212c6531bdb9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-312589/field-1/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46" :answer-field/choices ["q-312589/field-1/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46" "q-312589/field-1/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-312589" :question/id #uuid "c939b62d-8700-5d42-a705-6818a09c4515" :question/math-academy-id "q-312589" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=0$ since the logarithm is not defined here. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=3.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =2\\cdot 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2)+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{3-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-312589/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-312589"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342621/field-1/75be7144d0f06979df25a4d833026b673a3f8e67075b82357bb7a90b42abaa01" :answer/id #uuid "de19756b-6c9e-563b-9cb1-5a0b90131f73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342621/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "12d98025-b7f2-5a95-9b83-f1927db5e6a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342621/field-1/75be7144d0f06979df25a4d833026b673a3f8e67075b82357bb7a90b42abaa01" :answer-field/choices ["q-342621/field-1/75be7144d0f06979df25a4d833026b673a3f8e67075b82357bb7a90b42abaa01"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342621" :question/id #uuid "8b310eff-a1c1-5674-b489-414dfca2158d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342621" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\ge 1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =x-1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & =(x-1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & ={x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\ x+1 & =3({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\ x+1 & =3{x}^{2}-6x+3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-7x+2 & =0 \\\\ (3x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3},2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\ge 1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{2+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-342621/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342621"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-76106/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "0342f18f-0694-5143-a372-a4895c9aebbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76106/selection/219347bb4a6f51134d7f5aff5da33544f44afee934064621ebff9cf9e335ee59" :answer/id #uuid "34cb3e00-3c88-50ba-af4e-c0ca88bab101" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76106/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "99cd4340-3a82-5bec-a978-d7988c291072" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76106/selection/181d1f839d214e5807f4235aba03fc371467f433c702fbf48b29ccfdaa41b68d" :answer/id #uuid "936ab591-12a6-5713-a1da-87be5d9c6d15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76106/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "b1eba020-968d-5a9c-9f3d-795ffaaa5127" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76106/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f55768e6-67b0-5a70-91a9-fee053091581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-76106/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-76106/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-76106/selection/219347bb4a6f51134d7f5aff5da33544f44afee934064621ebff9cf9e335ee59" "q-76106/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" "q-76106/selection/181d1f839d214e5807f4235aba03fc371467f433c702fbf48b29ccfdaa41b68d" "q-76106/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-76106" :question/id #uuid "e0131ab8-e5fa-5085-adfc-84de0cb74b5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-76106" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{2x-1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(3).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(3)=x.$ For $x>\\frac{1}{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(3) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2x-1} & =3 \\\\ 2x-1 & =9 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(5),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{2x-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 5-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-76106/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-76106"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-110341/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "bae59a1b-3263-561a-9cd4-4449ef437b31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110341/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "58514938-c1e9-5d63-8917-72463ffc6022" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110341/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "a39e44b5-0bd0-593a-8fa1-67c1db7249ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110341/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer/id #uuid "1454d265-817d-5f47-9c10-7639b426a74d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110341/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" :answer/id #uuid "a2aaf225-8d73-5350-a42a-e69aa296da51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110341/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "126cafa6-7156-51f1-b07b-bb36ae50f873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-110341/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer-field/choices ["q-110341/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" "q-110341/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-110341/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-110341/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" "q-110341/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-110341" :question/id #uuid "d198f76c-2fe3-50e7-9351-8eafd2f01101" :question/math-academy-id "q-110341" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+5x+2.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+5x+2 & =2 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+5) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+5x+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\cdot 0+5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-110341/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-110341"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-312588/field-1/bd506038dfaf377fd54b4826acb0c04772328e8eb58834e9bfa3d8d2f8436aee" :answer/id #uuid "7b8e9a09-eb9e-504b-b42f-8c42ab38cdc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\ln(4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-312588/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0ac6665-8270-5b3f-aa0d-3ad34e91de23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-312588/field-1/bd506038dfaf377fd54b4826acb0c04772328e8eb58834e9bfa3d8d2f8436aee" :answer-field/choices ["q-312588/field-1/bd506038dfaf377fd54b4826acb0c04772328e8eb58834e9bfa3d8d2f8436aee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-312588" :question/id #uuid "a7e4d2ce-43e6-5f2f-a26a-1291eab1840c" :question/math-academy-id "q-312588" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={4}^{x}+2.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(6).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(6),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(6).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(6)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(6) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =6 \\\\ {4}^{x}+2 & =6 \\\\ {4}^{x} & =4 \\\\ {4}^{x} & ={4}^{1} \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(6)=1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={4}^{x}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & ={4}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-312588/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-312588"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-14720"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-14719"] :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2-3x}{4+5x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{22}{(4+5x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-22\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-22)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{22}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-14721"] :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x),$ and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain $f^{-1}(1)=2,$ $g(1)=-3,$ $g'(1)=1,$ and $f'(2)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(1) & =\\frac{g(1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}+{g}^{′}(1){f}^{-1}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(2)}+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-1}+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-14722"] :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
